      "content": "```markdown\n# Review of \"Quantum Coherence in Disordered Topological Insulators at Room Temperature\"\n\n## Abstract\nThe authors claim to have achieved sustained quantum coherence in a doped bismuth-based insulator at room temperature, which is a significant advancement in the field of topological insulators. However, there are concerns regarding their sample preparation methods and the noise floor in their microwave measurements.\n\n## Sample Preparation\nThe chemical vapor deposition (CVD) process followed by rapid thermal annealing is a standard approach, but the specifics of the dopant concentration, deposition temperature, and annealing conditions are not detailed. This lack of information makes it difficult to replicate their results and assess the reproducibility of their findings.\n\n## Measurement Techniques\nThe use of pulsed Electron Paramagnetic Resonance (EPR) spectroscopy is appropriate for measuring coherence, but the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of approximately 3:1 raises questions about the reliability of their data. The noise floor in their measurements could significantly impact the interpretation of their results, and it would be beneficial to include a more detailed analysis of the noise characteristics and potential sources.\n\n## Conclusion\nWhile the authors present an intriguing claim, the lack of detailed methodology and concerns about the noise",
